  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead was there to keep. In nine innings, they couldn't.",
    "what_this_means": "Griffin Canning lasted only 4.1 innings against Chicago, surrendering two runs on five hits before the bullpen took over. Kyle Hart, Bradgley Rodriguez, Adrian Morejon, Jason Adam, and Mason Miller combined for 2.7 scoreless frames. A clean bridge that kept it tied through eight. The bats moved 11 hits into the ballpark but converted just two of their runners in scoring position into runs. Inning six brought three chances with runners in scoring position. Inning eight, two more. Both critical. Both empty. That's the pattern: they manufactured traffic but never the traffic jam. Miguel Andujar went 3-for-4 and Xander Bogaerts drove in a run, but the lineup didn't answer when it mattered. The game turned in the ninth, after they'd blown their only opening, and now they're 11 back with three straight losses and a record that refuses to stabilize.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like from the inside. They had the resources to win. The starter held enough, the bullpen executed, the hits fell. What didn't fall was a single sequence that converted a runner at second into a run when the margin was still a choice instead of a grave. The offense produced in absolute terms and failed in the moments that counted, which is the season in miniature. Two runs on 11 hits is not a lineup problem in isolation; it is a leverage problem, and leverage problems are the ones that don't forgive close games late. They held the lead for two innings and gave it back in one. Eight left on base. Zero reward.",
